<section id="the-question" data-scene data-act="00" data-title="The Question" style="--scene-h:260vh">
  <div class="scene-stage">
    <div class="scene-inner">
      <h2 class="headline-sm" data-reveal data-from="0" data-to="0.12">In 2026, the question is no longer <em>can an LLM write good Python?</em></h2>
      <ul class="accent-trio-list" data-reveal data-from="0.2" data-to="0.3">
        <li class="beat accent-blue" data-reveal data-from="0.22" data-to="0.32">does it do what you actually wanted</li>
        <li class="beat accent-teal" data-reveal data-from="0.34" data-to="0.44">does it fit the whole project</li>
        <li class="beat accent-amber" data-reveal data-from="0.46" data-to="0.56">does it respect the constraints</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="beat-large" data-reveal data-from="0.68" data-to="0.82">When an agent writes bad code, it usually wasn&rsquo;t the model. It was missing instructions and context.</p>

      <aside class="notes">
        <p class="notes-label">Notes <span class="notes-pace">&asymp; 3 MIN</span></p>
        <div class="notes-body">
          <p>Read the START-HERE opening near-verbatim: &ldquo;Modern agentic coding systems are extremely good at following instructions. When you see them write bad code, it is probably not because the technology is bad, but instead because they didn&rsquo;t have sufficient instructions and context for the task.&rdquo;</p>
          <p>&ldquo;In 2026, the question is no longer, &lsquo;can an LLM write good Python?&rsquo; The question is whether the code does what you actually wanted, fits the whole project, and respects the constraints a senior engineer knows implicitly.&rdquo;</p>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- =====================================================================
     ACT I — GOOD CODE ISN'T ENOUGH  (≈5 min)
     ===================================================================== -->
<section id="failures" data-scene data-act="01" data-title="Good Code Isn&rsquo;t Enough" style="--scene-h:300vh">
  <div class="scene-stage">
    <div class="scene-inner scene-inner--wide">
      <p class="act-opener" data-reveal data-from="0" data-to="0.06">ACT I <span class="act-name">— GOOD CODE ISN&rsquo;T ENOUGH</span></p>
      <div class="card-grid" style="text-align:left">
        <div class="card" data-reveal data-from="0.14" data-to="0.24" data-dir="u">
          <p class="card-title">Built the wrong thing</p>
          <p class="card-dek">Solved a misunderstood problem.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card" data-reveal data-from="0.22" data-to="0.32" data-dir="u">
          <p class="card-title">Mis-sized it</p>
          <p class="card-dek">Over-engineered the simple, under-built the load-bearing.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card" data-reveal data-from="0.3" data-to="0.4" data-dir="u">
          <p class="card-title">Ignored a hard constraint</p>
          <p class="card-dek">Cost, scale, compliance.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card" data-reveal data-from="0.38" data-to="0.48" data-dir="u">
          <p class="card-title">Gold-plated</p>
          <p class="card-dek">Features nobody asked for.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="beat-large" style="margin-top:1.2em" data-reveal data-from="0.66" data-to="0.8">None of these are the model writing bad code. The code is probably fine.</p>

      <aside class="notes">
        <p class="notes-label">Notes <span class="notes-pace">&asymp; 5 MIN</span></p>
        <div class="notes-body">
          <p>From writing-a-vision, &ldquo;What a good vision prevents&rdquo;: an agent has no idea what your project is doing, who it is for, or what it should accomplish. If you ask it to go do a task blind, it will sometimes do it in a way that defeats the entire purpose of your repo. The fix is easy &mdash; tell your agent about your project.</p>
          <p>Agents will write excellent code that absolutely doesn&rsquo;t do what you wanted: building the wrong thing, because it solved a misunderstood problem and never saw the real goal; mis-sizing the solution, over-engineering something that should be simple or under-building something load-bearing; ignoring a hard constraint (cost, scale, compliance) it had no way of knowing existed; gold-plating features nobody asked for.</p>
          <p>None of these are the model writing bad code &mdash; the code is probably fine. They&rsquo;re the model writing code that&rsquo;s wrong for your project, because it never had the project&rsquo;s direction in front of it.</p>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- =====================================================================
     ACT II — YOUR THREE JOBS  (≈5 min)
     ===================================================================== -->
<section id="three-jobs" data-scene data-act="02" data-title="Your Three Jobs" style="--scene-h:260vh">
  <div class="scene-stage">
    <div class="scene-inner">
      <p class="act-opener" data-reveal data-from="0" data-to="0.06">ACT II <span class="act-name">— YOUR THREE JOBS</span></p>
      <ol class="card-stack">
        <li class="card" data-reveal data-from="0.16" data-to="0.28" data-dir="u">
          <span class="card-num">01</span>
          <span><span class="card-title">A capable model</span><span class="card-dek">Flagship &mdash; when in doubt, the most capable you have.</span></span>
        </li>
        <li class="card" data-reveal data-from="0.28" data-to="0.4" data-dir="u">
          <span class="card-num">02</span>
          <span><span class="card-title">A real harness</span><span class="card-dek">Specs, review, guardrails &mdash; not just a chat window.</span></span>
        </li>
        <li class="card" data-reveal data-from="0.4" data-to="0.52" data-dir="u">
          <span class="card-num">03</span>
          <span><span class="card-title">Context</span><span class="card-dek">Your vision, your architecture, your standards.</span></span>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <aside class="notes">
        <p class="notes-label">Notes <span class="notes-pace">&asymp; 5 MIN</span></p>
        <div class="notes-body">
          <p>From START-HERE: so when working with LLMs you have a few clear responsibilities &mdash; use a capable model that writes quality code; use a quality harness that writes specs, follows instructions, does code reviews, orchestrates sessions, and provides guardrails; and provide context to the model so it writes code that not only &ldquo;does the task&rdquo; but aligns with your project&rsquo;s vision, your codebase&rsquo;s architecture, and your software engineering standards.</p>
          <p>On the model, from harness-and-model: for most tasks that aren&rsquo;t tightly bounded, use a powerful flagship model, and when in doubt reach for the most capable one you have. We have the most experience with Claude Opus 4.8, but the latest flagships from OpenAI and Gemini drop in just as well.</p>
          <p>Don&rsquo;t chase leaderboards: the &ldquo;best&rdquo; model changes every few weeks, and the gap between the top flagships is almost never what makes or breaks a task. For small, mechanical, tightly-scoped work, a cheaper or faster model is often fine; for anything that needs real judgment or planning, use the strongest one available to you and give it access to as much quality context as you can.</p>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="harness" data-scene data-act="02" data-title="The Harness" data-anim="pipeline" data-pipe-a="0.14" data-pipe-b="0.78" style="--scene-h:320vh">
  <div class="scene-stage">
    <div class="scene-inner scene-inner--wide">
      <h2 class="headline-sm" data-reveal data-from="0" data-to="0.1">A raw model is a fancy next-word predictor.</h2>
      <ol class="pipeline pipeline--row">
        <li class="pipeline-step" data-step>
          <span class="pipeline-step-dot">1</span>
          <p>Spec first</p>
        </li>
        <li class="pipeline-step" data-step>
          <span class="pipeline-step-dot">2</span>
          <p>Implement to the spec</p>
        </li>
        <li class="pipeline-step" data-step>
          <span class="pipeline-step-dot">3</span>
          <p>Fresh-agent review</p>
        </li>
        <li class="pipeline-step" data-step>
          <span class="pipeline-step-dot">4</span>
          <p>Verify</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="beat" style="margin-top:1.4em" data-reveal data-from="0.84" data-to="0.96">Nothing moves forward until it checks out.</p>

      <aside class="notes">
        <p class="notes-label">Notes <span class="notes-pace">&asymp; 5 MIN</span></p>
        <div class="notes-body">
          <p>From harness-and-model, &ldquo;What a harness actually does for you&rdquo;: a raw model is a fancy next-word predictor. Give it a feature to build and it might start strong, drift halfway through, and then hand you something almost-but-not-quite right. A harness fixes that by turning &ldquo;build me this&rdquo; into a gated pipeline: each step is pinned to something concrete, and nothing moves forward until it checks out.</p>
          <p>Many on our team run a stack of harnesses: Claude Code on top of Opus 4.8, and then <a href="https://github.com/pcvelz/superpowers">pcvelz/superpowers</a> on top of that. The loop it runs: <strong>spec first</strong> &mdash; it explores your actual code, asks you questions, proposes an approach, and writes a design spec; nothing gets built until you approve it. <strong>Implement to the spec</strong> &mdash; an agent types out the code by following that spec, instead of improvising from your one-line request. <strong>Review by a fresh agent</strong> &mdash; a second agent that did not write the code reads the diff against the spec: every requirement met? anything quietly added? anything misunderstood? <strong>Verify</strong> &mdash; tests can be written ahead of the code, and the code isn&rsquo;t done until it passes them.</p>
          <p>You can run all of this in an isolated git worktree, so several tasks go at once without stepping on each other. You can also delegate portions to different models &mdash; for example the spec and the review to your most expensive and capable model while a cheaper, faster one does the typing in the middle. Superpowers does plenty more in the same spirit (systematic debugging before proposing fixes, structured handling of review feedback, a clean merge-and-cleanup step at the end). Install it with:</p>
          <p><code>/plugin marketplace add pcvelz/superpowers</code><br><code>/plugin install superpowers-extended-cc@superpowers-extended-cc-marketplace</code></p>
          <p><strong>Practical notes:</strong> run your agent on your actual machine, not in a browser tab &mdash; you want it in your real environment, with your tools and your repo, able to run and smoke-test what it builds. Harnesses and models both change fast; stay reasonably current, but don&rsquo;t agonize over it. The only rule that matters is to use a real harness and a capable model &mdash; which exact ones matter far less than the fact that you&rsquo;re using them at all.</p>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

